message.content:
Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how to obtain a **reactive** queue named `"taskQueue"` while explicitly supplying a custom JSON codec (`JsonJacksonCodec`).  
I’ll cover both the *new* `RedissonReactiveClient` API (which returns `RQueueReactive<T>`) and the older `RedissonRxClient` API (which returns `RQueue<T>` but still works reactively via RxJava).

---

## 1️⃣ Using the modern `RedissonReactiveClient`

```java
import org.redisson.Redisson;
import org.redisson.api.RQueueReactive;
import org.redisson.api.RedissonReactiveClient;
import org.redisson.config.Config;
import org.redisson.codec.JsonJacksonCodec;

// 1. Create a configuration (you can load from file, set single node, etc.)
Config config = new Config();
config.useSingleServer().setAddress("redis://127.0.0.1:6379");

// 2. Create the reactive client
RedissonReactiveClient reactiveClient = Redisson.createReactive(config);

// 3. Create your custom codec (here we use the built‑in JsonJacksonCodec)
JsonJacksonCodec jsonCodec = new JsonJacksonCodec();   // or new JsonJacksonCodec(MyCustomClass.class)

// 4. Obtain the reactive queue with the custom codec
RQueueReactive<MyTask> taskQueue = reactiveClient.getReactiveQueue("taskQueue", jsonCodec);

// 5. Example usage – enqueue a task
MyTask task = new MyTask("processOrder", 12345L);
taskQueue.offer(task)
          .doOnSuccess(v -> System.out.println("Task queued!"))
          .subscribe();

// 6. Example usage – dequeue a task
taskQueue.poll()
          .doOnSuccess(t -> System.out.println("Dequeued: " + t))
          .subscribe();
```

### What’s happening?

| Step | What it does |
|------|--------------|
| 1 | Builds a `Config` object pointing at your Redis instance. |
| 2 | Instantiates a **reactive** client (`RedissonReactiveClient`). |
| 3 | Creates a codec that knows how to serialize/deserialize your domain objects (`MyTask`). |
| 4 | Calls `getReactiveQueue(String name, Codec codec)` – this is the exact method you asked about. |
| 5–6 | Demonstrates how to push and pop items reactively. |

> **Tip:** If you’re only ever going to use JSON for all your data, you can set the codec globally in the `Config` (`config.setCodec(jsonCodec);`) and then simply call `client.getReactiveQueue("taskQueue")`. But passing the codec per‑call gives you fine‑grained control.

---

## 2️⃣ Using the legacy `RedissonRxClient` (RxJava 2)

If you’re on an older project that still uses RxJava 2, you can do essentially the same thing:

```java
import org.redisson.Redisson;
import org.redisson.api.RQueue;
import org.redisson.api.RedissonRxClient;
import org.redisson.config.Config;
import org.redisson.codec.JsonJacksonCodec;

// 1. Config
Config config = new Config();
config.useSingleServer().setAddress("redis://127.0.0.1:6379");

// 2. Create the Rx client
RedissonRxClient rxClient = Redisson.createRx(config);

// 3. Custom codec
JsonJacksonCodec jsonCodec = new JsonJacksonCodec();

// 4. Get the queue (note: returns RQueue<T>, not RQueueReactive<T>)
RQueue<MyTask> taskQueue = rxClient.getQueue("taskQueue", jsonCodec);

// 5. Example usage – enqueue
rxClient.getExecutorService("exec")
        .submit(() -> taskQueue.offer(new MyTask("sendEmail", 98765L)))
        .subscribe(result -> System.out.println("Enqueued via executor!"),
                   err -> err.printStackTrace());

// 6. Example usage – poll
taskQueue.poll()
        .subscribe(t -> System.out.println("Polled: " + t),
                   err -> err.printStackTrace());
```

> **Note:** With `RedissonRxClient`, the returned `RQueue` methods already return `Observable<T>`/`Single<T>` types, so you can chain them just like any other RxJava stream.

---

## 3️⃣ Quick checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| ✅ | Add Redisson dependency (`redisson-reactive` or `redisson-rxjava2`). |
| ✅ | Create a `Config` (or load from `redisson.yaml`). |
| ✅ | Instantiate the appropriate client (`Redisson.createReactive()` or `Redisson.createRx()`). |
| ✅ | Create or reuse a `Codec` instance (`JsonJacksonCodec`, `JsonJacksonCodec<MyTask>`, or your own implementation). |
| ✅ | Call `client.getReactiveQueue("taskQueue", myCodec)` (or `client.getQueue(...)` for Rx). |
| ✅ | Use the returned `RQueueReactive<T>` (or `RQueue<T>`) as a normal reactive stream. |

That’s all there is to it—just a single line of code to get the queue with your custom codec, wrapped in a fully reactive API. Happy coding!

